 Routine Linux CI uses pinned cargo-nextest 0.9.148 with the checked-in `ci` profile and all features, followed by serial `cargo test --doc --workspace --locked`; local `cargo test` remains supported. Windows and macOS run focused process, CLI library, installer and retrieval regressions with the lightweight default feature set and two test processes. The five native integration binaries share a one-slot nextest group; their internal concurrency and deadlines remain unchanged, while library cases can run in parallel. Existing process diagnostics are retained on failure. Full workspace Clippy/tests/doctests on those hosts are opt-in qualification. Feature-enabled tests exercise the helper; the runtime builder separately verifies the lightweight main and inference payload. Kernel inference/cache performance regressions now live in `crates/airk-kernel/tests/vector_performance.rs`: run that target with `--features local-inference -- --ignored --test-threads=1` and `AIRK_TEST_MODEL_DIR` set to the absolute directory of verified offline assets; Cargo starts integration tests from the crate directory. They are component measurements, separate from the native client and helper qualification scripts. CI builds/tests native binaries on Windows, macOS and Linux and checks the container recipe on Linux. Report what actually ran, including unavailable checks. The local Windows result alone does not qualify all platforms. See [CI and downloadable native candidates](docs/ci-and-builds.md) for the explicit architecture matrix, build credentials and spending controls.
 
